# ATEXIT(3)

## NAME

atexit - register a function to run at normal termination

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int atexit(void (*func)(void));
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Atexit* records *func* to be called when the program terminates normally. It returns 0 on
success and -1 if the registration could not be made - a null *func* or more than **32** of
them, which is C11 sec 7.22.4.2's own minimum and all this implementation offers.

The registered functions are called by exit(2), **in reverse order of registration,** and
**before** the standard I/O streams are flushed and closed, so a handler may still print. Each
is called with no arguments and its return value is discarded.

**It is not reached by every exit.** Nothing runs on _exit(2), on a signal that terminates the
process, or on a *sys exit* from the assembler, so it is a way of tidying up after a program's
own decision to stop, not after a crash. A handler that itself calls *exit* is undefined
behaviour in C11 and recurses here.

It is C11's and not v7's: a v7 program that wanted this wrote its own wrapper round *exit*, and
several in cmd/ still do.

## SEE ALSO

exit(2), fclose(3)

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/atexit.c
| lib/libc/gen/cuexit.c - the pointer *exit* tests

## BESM-6 NOTES

### Registering costs one word to everyone else

*Exit* is tail-jumped to by *crt0* and is therefore in every program. If it named the handler
table outright, every program would carry the 32 words of it, whether or not it had ever called
*atexit*. So *exit* tests a pointer that *atexit* arms - the same bargain fclose(3) makes for
the stdio flush, and for the same reason. *Hello* pays one word of bss for this page and
nothing else.

## BUGS

**The table is 32 entries and the limit is not diagnosed at compile time.** A thirty-third
registration simply returns -1, and a caller that ignores the result loses the handler
silently.
